{
  "abstract": "Gaussian integer digit supports offer superlinear candidate sets whose rotations escape scalar digit arguments. We prove that every stationary alphabet of at most six digits in the centered $3\\times3$ square, at radix $3+4i$, satisfies $\\alpha(S_k\\cap Q_n)\\leq1.79n+15552$ in every translated integer square and at every depth. Strong separation reduces all crop scales, origins and phases to a compact exact certificate. Its exhaustive run covers 22 symmetry classes, 90,112 phase cells and 145,330,790 crop nodes; a separate implementation checks one difficult phase per class. Joint coordinate-strip covers also exclude the eight-digit missing-corner support above $1.8n_k$ in its natural containing square for all $k\\geq10$. We then prove a general obstruction to final selections invariant under permutations of digits. A balanced-class pigeonhole argument places such selections in finitely many bounded-exception supports. Noninteger expansion radii give a power saving, integer radii with infinite rotations give uniform sublinear capacity using classical null-projection results, and finite rotations force eventually constant words. An explicit centered-grid identity gives the sharper exponent $\\log_5 4$. Finally, exact carry matrices show that elementary independent retention followed by one deletion per triple has a sublinear guarantee for all six-to-nine-digit centered alphabets. The limitation persists after conditioning on uniform digit frequencies and nearby rational types, by an exact stationary-flow certificate. A large midpoint-free energy slice distinguishes this calculation from correlated selection. The results restrict specified supports and symmetric selection rules; they do not bound unrestricted no-three sets or establish a construction above $1.8n$.",
